    'faq.title': 'Questions',
    'faq.macQ': 'My Mac says Apple “could not verify” the app is free of malware',
    'faq.macA': '<p>The app isn’t signed by an Apple developer account yet, so this shows once. Steps for macOS 15 (Sequoia) and later:</p><ol><li>Open the dmg and drag <strong>{app}</strong> into Applications.</li><li>Open it from Applications. When the warning appears, click <strong>Done</strong>, and don’t move it to the Trash.</li><li>Go to System Settings → <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to “Security” and click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>, then enter your password.</li><li>Click <strong>Open Anyway</strong> once more and your fairy appears.</li></ol><p>On older macOS you can right-click the app and choose “Open”. If it still won’t open, run this in Terminal and try again. It removes the “downloaded from the internet” flag:</p><pre><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/{app}.app</code></pre><p>If it says the app “is damaged and can’t be opened”, don’t move it to the Trash. Click <strong>Cancel</strong> and run the command above.</p><p>If you download a new version from this site and install it again, the warning shows once more. Open it the same way. After that, updates come through “Restart to update” inside the app.</p><p>Always move it to Applications before opening; auto-update won’t install while it runs from inside the dmg.</p>',
    'faq.winQ': 'Windows shows a blue “Windows protected your PC” screen',
    'faq.winA': '<p>That’s SmartScreen, shown for new unsigned apps. Click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>. It installs without admin rights and your fairy appears.</p><p>If a work computer blocks it with no “Run anyway” button, your administrator has locked this down, and it can’t be installed until a signed build is available.</p>',
    'faq.chipQ': 'Do I have an Apple chip or an Intel Mac?',
    'faq.chipA': '<p>Apple menu (top left) → <strong>About This Mac</strong>. If “Chip” starts with Apple M, it’s an Apple chip. If “Processor” says Intel, it’s Intel.</p>',
    'faq.noAiQ': 'Can I use it without any AI tools?',
    'faq.noAiA': '<p>Yes. Fairies, to-dos, notes and calendar all work without AI. To chat, choose “Free AI” in the chat window’s AI picker. It downloads a model once and then runs offline on your computer. Its answers are shorter and simpler than big models’.</p>',
    'faq.freeQ': 'Is it free?',
    'faq.freeA': '<p>Yes. The app is free and there’s no sign-up. Chatting through an AI tool like Claude Code follows that tool’s plan and usage. The free AI and photo-as-is fairies cost nothing.</p>',
    'faq.charQ': 'Can I make a fairy of a favorite character?',
    'faq.charA': '<p>With an AI connected, type a favorite character\'s name in "Describe it". Please enjoy fairies based on someone else\'s character personally, and don\'t sell them or spread them widely. If a rights holder asks, we\'ll act on it.</p>',
    'faq.coverQ': 'The fairy is getting in my way',
    'faq.coverA': '<p>Scrolling over the fairy passes through to the window underneath. Hide it for a moment with a shortcut, or turn off “Wander now and then” and make it smaller in Settings → Fairy. It hides by itself while you present or share your screen.</p>',
    'faq.updateQ': 'How do updates work?',
    'faq.updateA': '<p>On Mac and Windows the new version downloads in the background and your fairy offers “Restart to update”, and it installs only when you click. On Linux the fairy opens the releases page. You can turn update notices off in Settings → About.</p>',
    'faq.linuxQ': 'Does it run on Linux?',
    'faq.linuxA': '<p>As a preview. Unpack the <code>tar.xz</code> (<code>tar xJf</code>) and run <code>{file}</code> inside, or make the <code>AppImage</code> executable (<code>chmod +x</code>) and run it; some distros need <code>libfuse2</code>. Look together and perching on windows depend on your desktop environment.</p>',
    'faq.removeQ': 'How do I uninstall it?',
    'faq.removeA': '<p>On Mac, move {app} from Applications to the Trash; on Windows, remove it in Settings → Apps → Installed apps. Your to-dos and fairies stay, so reinstalling picks up where you left off. To erase everything, also delete the data folder:</p><ul><li>Mac <code>~/Library/Application Support/{file}</code></li><li>Windows <code>%APPDATA%\\{file}</code></li><li>Linux <code>~/.config/{file}</code></li></ul>',
    'faq.osQ': 'Which computers does it run on?',
    'faq.osA': '<p>Macs on macOS 13 (Ventura) or later (both Apple chip and Intel), and 64-bit PCs on Windows 10 or later. The Windows build is still being checked, and Linux x64 is a preview. Windows on ARM isn’t supported yet. Sizes are under <a href="{home}#download">Requirements &amp; sizes</a>.</p>',
    'faq.privQ': 'Do my to-dos leave my computer?',
    'faq.privA': '<p>{name} works fully without a server, so your to-dos, notes and calendar stay on this computer. Server features (an account, coin sync, server backup, chat passes and so on) are optional and used only by people who turn them on. People under 14 can’t turn them on. Things leave only when you use a feature that needs it, like AI chat. The <a href="{home}#privacy">privacy section</a> and the <a href="{home}privacy/">privacy policy</a> list exactly what goes where.</p>',
    'faq.oldQ': 'I used Jjakkung before',
    'faq.oldA': '<p>fairy is Jjakkung’s new name. Quit Jjakkung and open fairy for the first time. It brings over your to-dos, notes and settings by itself. After that you can uninstall Jjakkung. Old <code>.jjpet</code> files still open.</p>',

